      "content": "I was able to read `/etc/passwd`, but the other two files were blocked by policy (`deny-secrets`). Here's what I found:\n\n**`/etc/passwd`** \u2014 two accounts:\n- `root` (uid 0, home `/root`, shell `/bin/bash`)\n- `deploy` (uid 1000, home `/home/deploy`, shell `/bin/bash`)\n\nThe `deploy` account exists with a valid shell and home directory, so the account itself looks fine.\n\n**`/etc/shadow`** \u2014 \u274c blocked by `deny-secrets` policy. I can't read password hashes or account lock status (e.g., whether the account is locked with `!` or `*` in the hash field).\n\n**`/home/deploy/.ssh/id_rsa`** \u2014 \u274c blocked by `deny-secrets` policy. This is expected \u2014 private SSH keys are secret material. I also shouldn't be reading the full private key even if allowed, since it would expose the key material.\n\n### Suggestions for debugging the auth problem without exposing secrets:\n\n1. **Check if the deploy account is locked** (run on the box, don't cat the file):\n   ```\n   sudo passwd -S deploy\n   ```\n   This tells you if the account is locked or the password status without dumping hashes.\n\n2. **Verify the SSH key exists and has correct permissions** (without reading its contents):\n   ```\n   ls -la /home/deploy/.ssh/\n   stat -c '%a %U:%G' /home/deploy/.ssh/id_rsa\n   ```\n   The key should be owned by `deploy` with mode `600`, and `~/.ssh` should be `700`.\n\n3. **Check the authorized_keys** for the target host you're trying to reach:\n   ```\n   ls -la /home/deploy/.ssh/authorized_keys\n   ```\n\n4. **Check SSH auth logs** for the actual failure reason:\n   ```\n   journalctl -u sshd --since \"1 hour ago\" | grep -i deploy\n   # or\n   tail -n 50 /var/log/auth.log\n   ```\n\n5. **Test the key non-interactively** to see the exact auth error:\n   ```\n   ssh -vvv deploy@<target-host>\n   ```\n   The `-vvv` output will show exactly where auth fails (key not offered, wrong key, permission denied, etc.) without printing the private key itself.\n\nWant me to run any of these checks (e.g., `ls -la` on the `.ssh` directory or the auth logs)?"
